<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erzurum</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Theodosiopolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erzurum is a city on a high plateau in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, near the sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Known in late antiquity as Theodosiopolis, it guarded the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and was ruled in turn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, the Saltukids and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. The Congress of Erzurum of 1919 was an early step in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erzurum.jpg" alt="Erzurum" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zeynel Cebeci · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erzurum_Citadel_-_Erzurum_Kalesi_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Erzurum lies in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, 1,900 metres above sea level. It is the largest city and capital of Erzurum Province, and the site of ancient Theodosiopolis. Its Armenian name was Karin, and the present name arose after the Seljuk conquest that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071. When the neighbouring commercial city of Artze was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> in 1048/49, its Christian inhabitants moved to Theodosiopolis and called it Artsn Rum, Artze of the Rûm, meaning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, a name from which Erzurum is derived. The city has a double-headed eagle as its coat of arms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the Artaxiad and Arsacid kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, Karin was the capital of a canton in Upper Armenia. After Armenia was partitioned between the Eastern Roman Empire and Sassanid Persia in 387, the Romans fortified the city and named it Theodosiopolis after Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>. As the chief military stronghold on the empire's eastern border it was fiercely contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasius-i-dicorus">Anastasius I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> both strengthened its defences. The Umayyad general Abdallah ibn Abd al-Malik conquered it in 700 or 701, and it became the capital of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> and a base for raids into Byzantine territory. The Byzantine commander Theophilos Kourkouas captured it in 931 and again in 949, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> rebuilt its defences in 1018.</p><p>After 1071 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> held the city, and the Saltukids ruled an Anatolian principality centred on it from 1071 to 1202. Melike Mama Hatun, sister of Nâsırüddin Muhammed, was ruler from 1191 to 1200. The city fell to a Mongol siege in 1242 and was looted. It later belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> confederations, then to Safavid Persia, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> took it in 1514 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran">Battle of Chaldiran</a>. Nader Shah took it in 1733, and it returned to the Ottomans after his death in 1747. Russian forces occupied it in 1829, in 1878 and in 1916, and each time except the last it was returned.</p><p>The Armenian population of about 40,000 was deported and killed en masse during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a> of 1915, and when Russian forces took the city in 1916 scarcely 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were left alive. In 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a> resigned from the Ottoman army in Erzurum, and the Erzurum Congress of that year was one of the starting points of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Little of medieval Erzurum survives beyond scattered buildings, among them the citadel and the 13th-century Çifte Minareli Medrese, the Twin Minaret madrasa. Atatürk University, established in 1950, is among the largest universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and the nearby Palandöken Mountain draws visitors for winter sports. Small workshops make souvenirs from Oltu stone.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Erzurum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For three decades he extended his authority over neighbouring emirs and fought the Qara Qoyunlu, until he was killed in battle against them near <b>Erzurum</b> in 1435.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around them gathered the Saadlu, Karamanlu, Alpaut, Agacheri, Dukharlu, Jagirlu and Hajilu tribes, from Nakhchivan and Ganja to Maraş, <b>Erzurum</b> and Ardabil; their own traditions say they came to Anatolia from Central Asia with the Mongol conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…much: a fair, tall girl from the mountains (the Turkish version places the meeting in the market of <b>Erzurum</b> and names the nearby Palandöken mountain), a young man's cry of pain, the lovers kept apart, and the name Leyli, which recalls Layla, the famous beloved of Persian poetry.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sari-gelin">Sari Gelin</a></li><li><blockquote>Hayasa and Azzi were lands in the highlands of the upper Euphrates and the <b>Erzurum</b> region.</blockquote><a href="/state/azzi-hayasa">Azzi-Hayasa</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition holds that the Seljuk sultan Alp Arslan granted the region of <b>Erzurum</b> to the commander Saltuk after the victory at Manzikert in 1071.</blockquote><a href="/state/saltukids">Saltukids</a></li></ul></section>
